{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="content" %}
# Content design

## The video follows a logical that directly supports its learning uuobjectives

Organise the video around explicit intended learning outcomes. Each section, explanation, example and visual should contribute to what learners are expected to know or perform. Remove content that is interesting but unnecessary. This applies the coherence principle: learners generally perform better when extraneous information is excluded.[^mayer]

Use a structure appropriate to the subject rather than enforcing a rigid template. A useful default may follow [BOPPPS][boppps-model] or [CARD][card-model] model and consist of a brief orientation, clearly identified sections, explicit transitions and a final synthesis or application prompt. 

Break complex explanations into meaningful segments and allow learners to process one segment before moving to the next. A meta-analysis found small-to-moderate positive effects of learner-paced segmentation on retention and transfer.[^segmenting] Guidance for educational-video production similarly recommends keeping videos focused on their learning goals and using signals to highlight important concepts.[^video-guidelines]

[^mayer]: [The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning (Chapter 14) - The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning")

[^segmenting]: [Meta-analysis of the segmenting effect](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-018-9456-4 "Rey, G. D., Beege, M., Nebel, S., Wirzberger, M., Schmitt, T. H., & Schneider, S. (2019). A Meta-analysis of the Segmenting Effect. Educational Psychology Review, 31, 389–419.")

[^video-guidelines]: [Effective educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1187/cbe.16-03-0125 "Brame, C. J. (2016). Effective Educational Videos: Principles and Guidelines for Maximizing Student Learning from Video Content. CBE—Life Sciences Education, 15(4), es6.")


## The length of the video is meaningful and motivated (~6 minutes sweetspot)

Shorter videos boost engagement and learning. A large-scale MOOC study found median viewing times ≈6 minutes, regardless of longer actual length[^mooc], and another study showed students watching short (~8 min) videos scored 9% higher than those watching ~55 min lectures.

Brief videos match attention spans better (studies indicate drop-offs after 6-9 min) and encourage active re-engagement. Quality Matters and other experts recommend keeping lessons under ~6-10 min. Short clips can be easily rewatched, supplemented with activities and reused in other learniung paths.

[^mooc]: [Study of MOOC videos](https://doi.org/10.1145/2556325.2566239 "How video production affects student engagement: An empirical study of MOOC videos. Philip J. Guo, Juho Kim, Rob Rubin. School of Computing Massachusetts Institute of Technology, University of Rochester & EdX")

## Branded intro/outro is not longer than 3 seconds

Keep any intros/outros extremely short. Use minimal animations or a logo flash of 1-2 seconds if needed. Very short intros avoid losing attention. Viewers generally "want to jump right into content". 

Minimising non-instructional sequences eliminates unnecessary delays and prevents early drop-off. Quick branding (e.g. a flash of a logo) can provide professionalism without boredom. Especially when your course contain 30+ videos with extly the same intro & outro in each.

A brilliant example of the extremely short but still speaking out intro in instructional video is the Google Career Certification course on Coursera.[^google-courses]

[^google-courses]: [Google Career Certification Course Example](https://www.coursera.org/learn/foundations-data/lecture/aA1Wv/welcome-to-the-google-data-analytics-certificate "Foundations: Data, Data, Everywhere. A part of Google Data Analytics Professional Certificate. Coursera")

## Text intro is addressed to the targeted learner group

